    <section class="story section-grid" id="why-tsoi">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="story-layout">
          <div class="founder-portrait">
            <img src="assets/ankit-profile.webp" alt="Ankit Pushpraj, Co-Founder, The Schools of India" width="560" height="560" loading="lazy">
            <div class="portrait-caption"><strong>Ankit Pushpraj</strong><span>Co-Founder, The Schools of India</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="story-copy">
            <div class="eyebrow"><span></span> Founder’s Note</div>
            <h2>What Will Education Look Like <em>in 2040?</em></h2>
            <p>A child who walks into Class 1 this year will finish school in the late 2030s. By then, the world they step into may look nothing like the one we are preparing them for.</p>
            <p>Information that once took a library visit now takes a second. Artificial intelligence can write an essay, solve an equation and explain a concept in any language. Careers that will define the next decade do not have names yet. So the question for every school in India is no longer only “Are we teaching well?” It is “Are we teaching for the world these children will actually live in?”</p>
            <p>At The Schools of India Summit, we want to ask that question honestly, and together. What does a teacher become when knowledge is everywhere? How should we assess a child when answers are one click away? Should schooling still take fifteen years? And in a future full of technology, how do we protect what matters most in a school: the relationship between a child and an adult who believes in them?</p>
            <p>We do not have all the answers, and nobody does. But the schools that will serve children best in 2040 are being shaped by decisions made today, in staffrooms, in principals’ offices and in founders’ boardrooms across the country. That is why this summit brings founders, school leaders, teachers and students into the same room. The future of education should not be designed for children without the people who teach them, and without the children themselves.</p>
            <p>Our belief is simple. Every child can flourish, if the adults around them are willing to rethink what school is for. This January in Pune, we invite every school leader and educator who is ready to have that conversation to join us.</p>
            <blockquote>“The future of Indian education is not something we wait for. It is something we build.”</blockquote>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="section-heading benefits-heading">
          <div>
            <div class="eyebrow"><span></span> Benefits</div>
            <h2>Why You Should Be Here</h2>
          </div>
          <p>Not just a goodie bag. Not just an award. Six things worth carrying back to your school the next morning.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-grid">
          <article class="benefit-card accent-magenta"><span class="card-number">01</span><h3>Practical ideas</h3><p>Not frameworks in theory - practices other schools have already tested, refined, and are willing to hand over exactly as they run them.</p></article>
          <article class="benefit-card accent-violet"><span class="card-number">02</span><h3>The TSOI Playbook</h3><p>A working document built live across the day - every SOP and framework that surfaced in the room, yours to adapt long after the summit ends.</p></article>
          <article class="benefit-card accent-lime"><span class="card-number">03</span><h3>One problem, a concrete solution</h3><p>Walk in stuck on something specific. Walk out with either a sharper way to think about it, or the name of someone who's already solved it.</p></article>
          <article class="benefit-card accent-cyan"><span class="card-number">04</span><h3>5-10 real relationships</h3><p>Not LinkedIn connections made in a coffee queue - the kind built over a day spent solving the same problems in the same room. People you'll actually call.</p></article>
          <article class="benefit-card accent-magenta"><span class="card-number">05</span><h3>A sharper question</h3><p>The question you walked in with won't survive a day of pushback from people who've built what you're trying to build. You'll leave with a better one.</p></article>
          <article class="benefit-card accent-violet"><span class="card-number">06</span><h3>One experiment</h3><p>Paired with someone in the room to keep you honest, you leave with exactly one change to run the week you're back - not a list of forty you'll never open again.</p></article>
        </div>
        <div class="section-cta"><a class="btn btn-dark" href="#final-form">Request Call Back <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
      </div>
    </section>

